on as the product expands.</p></li></ul><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Rapidly iterate based on operator, engineering, and customer feedback.</p></li></ul><ul style="min-height:1.5em"><li><p style="min-height:1.5em">Fill in reasonable UX details independently rather than requiring every interaction to be explicitly specified.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p></li></ul><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>What Great Looks Like</strong></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You receive a well-written product spec and a description of the required Figma frames.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You understand the intent quickly.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You identify the handful of questions that actually matter.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">Then you disappear for a period of focused work and come back with something substantially better than what was described.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">The resulting Figma should be clear enough that engineering can build from it, polished enough to show a customer, and thoughtful enough that the Head of Product does not need to design every interaction personally.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em"></p><p style="min-height:1.5em"><strong>The Person We’re Looking For</strong></p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You are an unusually strong individual contributor.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You are fast.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You have excellent product judgment and visual judgment.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You are comfortable designing dense, information-rich software rather than only simple consumer applications.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You enjoy receiving strong product direction and turning it into an excellent finished experience.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You are comfortable making decisions independently when requirements do not specify every detail.</p><p style="min-height:1.5em">You can distinguish between a product question that requires escalation and a design detail that 